\usepackage{booktabs}

\title{Summer Stream Temperatures at Three Riffle Sites in the
Wye Catchment: A Volunteer Monitoring Note}
\author{D.~Pryce and N.~Halloran}
\date{September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Volunteer loggers recorded water temperature at three riffle sites
in the upper Wye catchment through four weeks of the early summer
spate season. Weekly means rose steadily at all sites, with the
lowest site consistently warmest. A logger lost in a spate left a
two-week gap at one site; the gap is reported as missing rather
than estimated.
\end{abstract}

\section{Monitoring Setup}
Three logging thermistors were anchored at riffle sites along the
upper Wye: Riffle A at the head of the study reach, Riffle B two
kilometres downstream, and Riffle C at the foot of the reach. The
loggers recorded every quarter hour. The Riffle B logger was torn
from its anchor during a spate in the third week and could not be
recovered until the following month, so no readings exist for the
gap. Cells in Table~\ref{tab:temps} are left blank rather than
estimated.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Site & Week 1 & Week 2 & Week 3 & Week 4 \\
\midrule
Riffle A & 14.2 & 14.8 & 15.1 & 15.6 \\
Riffle B & 14.5 & 14.9 & --   & --   \\
Riffle C & 15.0 & 15.4 & 15.8 & 16.1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Weekly mean water temperature in degrees Celsius}
\label{tab:temps}
\end{table}

\section{Reading the Table}
All three sites warmed by roughly one and a half degrees over the
four weeks, and the downstream sites ran consistently warmer than
the head of the reach. The gap at Riffle B breaks the site-by-site
comparison for the second half of the period; we report the
remaining sites without adjustment.

\section{Next Season}
Spare loggers and a second anchor point at each site are planned
for next season, and the catchment trust's own hydrometry team has
offered access to its backup records for cross-checking gaps.

\end{document}
